# Diagnosis: `paper-repo` incumbent

## Scope and evidence

This diagnosis uses the frozen target `rw_e584f68c0d9c` monitor stream and its final release/review events. The incumbent is Codex `gpt-6.1-sol` at low reasoning effort. The relevant baseline execution is `paper-repo` turn `db15a418` (18:48:31–18:54:14 UTC, 342 seconds, 11 model requests, 4 execute calls), followed by review/merge turns `c44f3caa`, `5a6e7d32`, and `82abf45a`.

## Worker-level finding

**The worker did not maintain a single verified provenance record for the exact committed download.** In `db15a418`, the first download command at 18:49:13 fetched the NOAA CSV and captured a timestamp; it then wrote `report/report.md` at 18:50:11. After review activity, at 18:52:13 it downloaded the CSV again and pushed commit `ffcbe4c` (“Correct NOAA fetch timestamp provenance”). The next recorded edit at 18:53:11 ran `sed -i 's/2026-10-25T18:52:23Z/2026-10-25T18:52:14Z/' ...` and pushed `28f3e7f` (“Use completed NOAA fetch time”). The stream therefore shows a report timestamp that had to be corrected twice, including a value (`18:52:23Z`) that postdated the completed fetch (`18:52:14Z`). This is an execution/verification failure attributable to the worker's process, not to a missing world resource: the network worked and the worker had shell access to `date`, `sha256sum`, and the downloaded file.

The same turn opened a separate “Analysis and report commitment” issue after inspecting the data and made several review/status checks before the final PR. Those actions are valid collaboration, but they consumed 4 paper-worker turns and 109 model seconds for a small one-paragraph report. The record supports a priority to make the fetch/hash/report check atomic and to verify once before review; it does not by itself prove that higher effort improves the final text.

## What the worker did well

The worker downloaded the required NOAA URL, preserved `data/co2_annmean_mlo.csv`, computed the last-year and ten-year values with Python, produced `report/report.md`, supplied the source file for internal review, and completed release approval. The final internal and external review events state that the one-paragraph report contained the URL, completed fetch timestamp, SHA-256, endpoint value, comparator, and ten-year rate. The run is therefore a successful baseline observation with avoidable corrective work, not a failed deliverable.

## Layer separation

The absence of a judging contract is a world/position gap recorded in the objective: no judging was supplied. It is not evidence that the worker is wrong. The target world also had a review workflow that prompted a second fetch and timestamp correction; that workflow belongs to the world/position layer. The worker-level finding above is limited to the worker's failure to bind the timestamp to the exact file it ultimately committed and to verify the edited report before review.

## Candidate implication and what remains

The tuner has proposed one candidate, `medium-v1`, changing only `codex.model_reasoning_effort` from low to medium. No evidence yet shows a quality difference. The candidate must be run against the same diagnostic/held-out starts and judged with the proposed field-level checks. If both workers produce the required report, compare correction count, provenance consistency, arithmetic, paragraph/no-figure constraints, and model seconds; retain the incumbent if medium effort does not improve the judged result within the 8-turn/900-second run limits.

