## Matched diagnostic result

The resumed incumbent (`rw_06c32490a8b3`, low effort) and candidate (`rw_87bae9dc4605`, medium effort) started from the same `final-0e3e7fb9` checkpoint with the same task and contract. Both preserved the report and CSV and recorded the exact source/hash/value parser check (incumbent monitor event 21:54:49 in `f14e7c79`; candidate monitor event 22:01:50 in `62a52949`). The incumbent ended at commit `57172b7`, merged the approved release PR and opened final PR #10; the candidate ended at `551b16a`, with release PR #9 approved only after its paper worker had ended. The two worlds consumed 246.8 and 247.7 model seconds respectively and both exhausted all 8 executions.

This is not a quality difference. Both worlds report `runtime_reason=budget-exhausted`, `release_status=unverified`, and the same explicit gap, “Driver termination alone does not establish scientific or delivery completion.” The difference in whether the approval arrived before the last paper turn is scheduler timing: the candidate's internal approval appears at 22:06:53 after its 22:06:06 worker completion, while the incumbent's approval at 22:06:51 woke a final paper turn that completed at 22:07:16. Provider overload retries (four incumbent requests, three candidate requests) are also shared runtime conditions. These are world/position problems and are excluded from worker diagnosis.

The only worker-level defect established by the completed target baseline remains the two timestamp corrections described above: the low-effort worker fetched again and wrote `18:52:23Z`, then corrected it to the completed fetch time `18:52:14Z`. The matched diagnostic task began from the already corrected final checkpoint, so it did not re-expose that defect. Medium effort has no demonstrated quality gain or regression on the matched task; retain the incumbent unless a held-out judged task supplies a real difference. Any claim that either worker completed the objective must be rejected because neither world produced an approved release.

## Final put-back diagnosis

The kept incumbent was put back in fresh continuation `rw_f0c559601c44` from `rw_e584f68c0d9c` checkpoint `final-0e3e7fb9`. After a first eight-execution drive stopped with two workflow handoffs open, a second bounded drive completed the same world: the terminal monitor reports an accepted, verified final release (`result:final` commit `69235c735d9fee7b32567d01711e565ea110a831`), complete delivery and review gates, no failures, and the required report and preserved CSV artifacts. This confirms that the kept worker can complete the target contract when the workflow receives the extra bounded drive needed for the approval/final-publication handoffs.

The extra drive is a world/position-layer scheduling requirement, not a newly observed worker defect: the first drive ended at its eight-execution boundary immediately after review approval created the final delivery obligation, and the second drive discharged it. The raw status lifecycle retains the earlier unverified value while the current monitor summary and artifact manifest are verified; this is instrumentation state and is excluded from worker diagnosis. The worker-level conclusion is unchanged: the baseline provenance timestamp corrections remain the only observed defect, and medium effort has no measured quality difference against low effort. The incumbent therefore remains the evidence-supported worker to release.
